<section id="abstraction" class="chapter"><div class="chapter-title"><span class="index">09</span><div><h2>Abstract after the concrete case</h2><p class="deck">Traits express behavior. They do not require a framework.</p></div></div>
<p>A trait defines shared behavior. An impl supplies it for a type. A generic bound or argument-position <code>impl Trait</code> lets the compiler specialize code for a concrete type. <code>dyn Trait</code> dispatches dynamically through a pointer, such as a reference or a <code>Box</code>. Not every trait is dyn-compatible.</p>
<Example name="trait" />
<p>Use <code>mod</code> for module structure, <code>use</code> for bringing names into scope, and <code>pub</code> deliberately at an API boundary. A library in src/lib.rs makes core logic reusable and easy to test; src/main.rs can concentrate on arguments, I/O, and exit behavior.</p>
<article class="wisdom"><p class="eyebrow">Practitioner note / Matthias Endler</p><h3>Write code a tired colleague can trace.</h3><p>Endler's “Be Simple” challenges unnecessary generics and indirection. The practical lesson is to make the domain operation visible and introduce abstraction when it resolves a concrete need. In this project, one parser and a small Score type are enough. A universal parsing framework would hide more than it explains.</p><p class="application">Apply it: before adding a trait, name the second implementation or the boundary it isolates.</p></article>
